  "emitters_from": "ZzzObject.cpp, RenderObject, case WD_3NORIA: a BITMAP_LIGHT sprite at bone(s) 61, 62, 63, 64, 65, drawn at scale 1.0 in Luminosity * (1.0, 1.0, 1.0). The positions are those bones solved out of this model's own skeleton, in MU units, which is what `at` is in.\n\nThe breathing is the client's own and is shared by every glow in the town: g_Luminosity = sin(WorldTime * 0.004) * 0.15 + 0.6 (SceneManager.cpp:1283), so it travels between 0.45 and 0.75 at 0.637 Hz. MU re-rolls it per frame; this smooths it, for the reason Lamps gives - the same number applied to a light rather than to a texture is a strobe on the ground.\n\nThe range is the one invention. MU's is a sprite: it has a size on screen and lights nothing, so there is no reach to copy. It is taken off the sprite's own scale so the small ones stay small, and it is the number to change if the elf town reads too wet.",
  "additive": [
    "yellow_jewel"
  ],
  "additive_from": "ZzzObject.cpp, MoveObject, case WD_3NORIA: `o->BlendMesh = 1` for type 39, set every frame rather than at creation, which is why the CreateObject transcription missed it. Mesh 1 is yellow_jewel.",
  "geared": {
    "bones": [
      0,
      3
    ],
    "loops": 4
  },
  "geared_from": "A bench choice, not MU's, and marked as one. The clip is MU's and so is its rate - the 0.16 every world object carries, nothing in WD_3NORIA overriding it - and in it the goblins crank once while the drum (bones 0 and 3, its two halves) turns a full revolution, eight keys of 45 degrees. On screen that read as goblins shuffling beside a drum that whirled, and halving the whole clip only swapped which half looked wrong. So the drum takes four cranks to go round once: goblins at MU's two seconds, drum at eight seconds a turn, Karlis's call on 2026-09-12.\n\nOnly the drum. The wheels at the goblins' end (bones 6 and 9) are the ones in their hands, and were geared with the drum at first - which left the goblins cranking a wheel turning a quarter as fast as their arms. They stay on the action's clock, and the chain between them is what gears the drum down. Remove this to restore MU's gearing. See write_animations.",
